---
import {type Goal, NEXT_RACE} from "../lib/constants";
import {iconClass} from "../lib/icons";

interface Props {
    goal: Goal;
}

const {goal} = Astro.props;

/*
 * THE WAY OUT OF THE GOAL CARD, AND NOTHING ELSE.
 *
 * It replaces a bordered chip that was doing two jobs at once: reporting how long until
 * the next race AND being the link to the wall. Splitting them is what let each one get
 * smaller. The countdown is now an ordinary line of the card's figures column, not
 * interactive at all, and this says where it goes — which is also the only thing a
 * control should say.
 *
 * IT IS A STYLED CONTROL NOW, `control-cta`, AND THE HISTORY BELOW IS WHY IT TOOK TWO GOES.
 *
 * Two friends reviewing the site did not know this could be pressed. The measurement said
 * why: at 1024x600 this element and the figure line directly above it were both
 * rgb(250,250,250) at 12px — a contrast ratio of 1.00:1 between a link and a sentence —
 * with a 13px chevron the only thing telling them apart. Both were reading on a phone,
 * where the hover colour cannot happen at all.
 *
 * The first fix named the idiom `text-link` and underlined it. That closed the half of the
 * defect that says "this is a link" and left the other half open: on a card whose one job
 * is a figure, the way out was still a run of words the reader had to notice. This closes
 * that half by making it the card's one ACTION and drawing it the way this site draws an
 * action — the accent border, the offset plate, the press.
 *
 * THE REASON THIS FILE USED TO GIVE FOR HAVING NO BOX HAS NOT BEEN OVERRIDDEN, IT HAS BEEN
 * SPENT. It read: "the offset plate is this site's mark for a 48px styled control, and
 * wearing it on a 24px text link would either dilute the mark or claim a size this is not."
 * Every clause is still true. It forbids plating a 24px run of words, and this is a 48px
 * styled control, so it no longer reaches. Do not restore it against a later proposal
 * without checking which of the two things is being drawn.
 *
 * IT IS NO LONGER THE SAME OBJECT AS THE WALL'S "Home" LINK, and that is a real loss taken
 * deliberately. The mirror was: an inline row, the card's own text colour, a glyph, the
 * accent on hover. What survives of it is the pairing that carries the meaning — this
 * leaves with `ri:arrow-right-line` and the wall comes back with `ri:arrow-left-line`, so a
 * reader still meets a matched pair. What is gone is the drawing, and the asymmetry is
 * honest: this is the one action on a card of figures, where the wall's Home link sits in a
 * header beside a heading and a row of sport chips. Giving that one a plate too would put a 48px
 * control in a place that has no action to offer.
 *
 * FULL WIDTH IS THE HIT BOX ARGUMENT INVERTED. The old note here recorded that a column
 * flex container stretches its items, so an unpinned anchor's box was the full 182px of the
 * card for 115px of ink — 67px of blank card that navigated when clicked — and it closed
 * that with `align-self: start`. The box is deliberately the full width again, and the
 * defect does not return, because the box is now DRAWN: a reader can see exactly where the
 * target begins and ends. That is the bib's own idiom (Patch.astro) — the whole object is
 * the anchor and it looks like one. The alternative was measured and rejected on the same
 * grounds it would have been chosen for: a 153px content-width button leaves 29px of dead
 * card beside it, which is the ragged edge, not the tidy one.
 *
 * THE LABEL IS STILL THE WHOLE ACCESSIBLE NAME. These words name the destination with the
 * very string that page is headed with (see [...sport].astro), so there is no sr-only
 * completion and nothing to append — tests/build-output.test.ts asserts the pairing in both
 * directions. Note what that rules out: an icon-only version of this control. It was built
 * and rendered, and it is the one shape where the label stops being on screen and the glyph
 * has to carry a destination no single glyph can name.
 *
 * SC 2.5.5 IS THE FLOOR NOW, NOT SC 2.5.8. The target used to be the ink plus the glyph and
 * a 1.5rem floor held it at the 24px AA minimum. It is a declared 48px box, which clears the
 * 44x44 AAA target as well — the same number every other styled control on this site meets,
 * and for the same reason (see the box's arithmetic in uno.config.ts).
 *
 * THE GLYPH IS 1em AND CARRIES NOTHING BUT ITS UTILITY AND ITS PIN, which is a change the
 * suite asked for rather than a preference. It shipped at `1.15em` from a class of its own
 * while this was a text link, and `tests/control-geometry.test.ts` allows a control's icon
 * span exactly its `i-` utility, `shrink-0`, and the toggle's theme selector — an allowlist,
 * because a width utility and a display utility both defeated the property scan it replaced
 * (they are named in that file, which the extractor does not read). An authored `font-size`
 * on the span is the same kind of thing those two were, so the size now comes from the
 * control's own type and the pin is the utility the allowlist names. The difference on
 * screen is 13.8px of arrow against 12; the gate is worth more than 1.8px.
 *
 * WHAT IS AUTHORED HERE AND WHY. The height, the surface and the box all come from the
 * shortcut, so what is left below is only the two things a shortcut cannot say: the
 * keyboard focus, and what the whole control does in forced colours. The `min-height` floor that
 * used to be the point of these rules is gone with the box that needed it —
 * tests/rendered-html.test.ts still reads every rule whose selector names this class and
 * refuses a length in device pixels, and the shortcut's height is 3rem, so the guarantee is
 * unchanged.
 *
 * (Two of the sentences above are worded around utility names on purpose. UnoCSS extracts
 * from .astro text, so the obvious spellings of "3rem height", "a repaired defect", "a row
 * of sport chips" and "a focus outline" each emit a real CSS rule that no element wears, and
 * tests/build-output.test.ts fails the build by name when they do. All four were caught here
 * on the first run.)
 */
const label = NEXT_RACE.control.replace("{sport}", goal.goal_name.toLowerCase());
---

<a
        class="events-link control-cta"
        href={`/patches/${goal.sport}`}
><span class="events-link-label">{label}</span><span
        class={`${iconClass(NEXT_RACE.icon)} shrink-0`} aria-hidden="true"></span></a>

<style>
    /*
     * A BOX COSTS SOMETHING A RUN OF WORDS DID NOT, AND THIS IS WHERE IT IS PAID.
     *
     * The browser sweep found the first draft of this control clipping its own label under
     * text-only zoom. Ink lost past the card's right edge at 1024x797, by root font-size:
     *
     *     root      16   20   24   28   32   36    40
     *     was        0    0    0    0    0    0     0    the run of words this replaced
     *     draft      0    0    0    0    0  12.7  42.2   a 3rem box, bare text node
     *     ships      0    0    0    0    0    0     0    the three fixes below
     *
     * The cause is width, and it is structural rather than a slip: a button spends horizontal
     * padding, a gap and a 1em arrow on chrome, and the card gets NARROWER as the root grows
     * because its own padding is font-relative too. At a 32px root the card leaves 110px and
     * the chrome wants 40 of them. A run of words spent nothing and always had the full 110.
     *
     * THREE THINGS FIX IT AND EACH ONE IS NECESSARY:
     *
     *   1. `min-width: 0` on the label, below. A bare text node in a flex container is an
     *      ANONYMOUS flex item whose `min-width: auto` resolves to a whole word, and no
     *      selector can reach it — which is the only reason the label is wrapped in an element.
     *      Without this the other two do nothing: the item is never offered a narrow width.
     *   2. `overflow-wrap: break-word` beside it, for the word that still does not fit the
     *      width it was just narrowed to.
     *   3. `flex-wrap` and a height FLOOR on the shortcut, so the arrow can drop below the
     *      label and the box can get taller instead of clipping. With the children pinned to
     *      one line the same label came out 418px tall at a 32px root against 202 now; with a
     *      pinned height it was clipped outright.
     *
     * WHAT IS NOT FIXED, stated plainly: at a 32px root — WCAG 1.4.4's 200% — this control is
     * 202px tall where the run of words was 96. No content is lost and the page scrolls, so
     * the criterion is met, but the box is the more expensive object at that setting and the
     * honest reason to accept it is that it is the cheaper one at 16px, where readers are.
     */
    .events-link-label {
        /* Let the label narrow to whatever the button has left. An anonymous flex item —
           which is what a bare text node becomes — has `min-width: auto`, resolving to a
           whole word, and no selector can reach it to say otherwise. That is the only
           reason the label is wrapped in an element at all. */
        min-width: 0;
        overflow-wrap: break-word;
    }

    /*
     * A VISIBLE KEYBOARD FOCUS, which this element did not have and neither does any other
     * control on the site — every one of them rides the browser's default outline. That
     * default is a real indicator, so this is an improvement rather than a repair, and it is
     * authored here rather than added to the shortcut on purpose: making it the shortcut's
     * business would change the drawing of seven other controls in a change that was asked
     * to alter two, and that is the owner's call to make separately.
     *
     * The offset is what keeps it legible: the plate sits 2px down and right of the box, so
     * an outline drawn ON the border is half-covered by it at the two edges that matter.
     */
    .events-link:focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
        outline-offset: 2px;
    }

    /*
     * Forced colours discard the accent and the text token alike. The border survives — a
     * forced mode paints its own — so unlike the run of words this replaces, the control is
     * no longer identified by colour alone even without these rules. They stay because
     * `LinkText` is the colour the mode reserves for exactly this, and because losing one of
     * two cues is still a loss. It is a system colour rather than a token, so it is set here
     * instead of among the themes.
     */
    @media (forced-colors: active) {
        .events-link {
            color: LinkText;
        }

        /*
         * WITHOUT THIS THE ARROW DISAPPEARS IN FORCED COLOURS.
         *
         * The mechanism is presetIcons': an icon class paints its artwork as a MASK over
         * `background-color: currentColor`, and `background-color` is one of the properties a
         * forced-colours mode overrides. The glyph therefore paints Canvas on Canvas and
         * vanishes. Measured on the built page with the mode emulated, back when this was a
         * chevron on an unboxed link: glyph `rgb(255,255,255)` on a card of `rgb(255,255,255)`
         * — a ratio of exactly 1.
         *
         * `forced-color-adjust: none` opts the glyph out of the override; the explicit
         * LinkText is what it paints instead. Same technique as ProgressBar.astro's fill.
         */
        .events-link span {
            forced-color-adjust: none;
            background-color: LinkText;
        }
    }
</style>
